s=s.replace(s.splitlines()[0],'# Lineage structure and molecular diversity of microbial strategies across terrestrial Mars analogues',1)
start=s.index('Microbial life in terrestrial');end=s.index('\n\nKeywords:',start)
s=s[:start]+'''Microorganisms in terrestrial Mars analogue environments must maintain cellular integrity while acquiring energy and carbon. Whether shared strategy labels correspond to similar genetic repertoires remains unresolved. We compared lineage structure and source-associated variation across seven metagenome-assembled genome catalogues, examined specified gene combinations in an additional dryland catalogue and characterized an AI-prioritized protein family outside the assigned KO repertoire. Of 4,213 original genomes, 3,904 passed primary quality thresholds. Under source-preserving null models, 42 of 43 variable bacterial maintenance markers and 14 of 15 energy markers were phylogenetically clustered. Maintenance and energy portfolios differed in source association, but their paired difference was uncertain under lineage-block Bayesian reweighting and sensitive to molecular composition. Follow-up analyses resolved unequal associations among specific systems, including potassium acquisition and regulation. In stringent dryland genomes, 35 of 38 KdpABC co-detections formed same-strand clusters within 10 kb, while limited cross-site lineage overlap constrained ecological replication. The DPFQ008 case comprised 63 proteins with conserved cytochrome-c-like features and lineage-associated sequence variation, supporting an electron-transfer-related hypothesis without resolving biochemical function. Together, these results distinguish shared strategy labels from the lineage-associated molecular repertoires that encode them. They provide a comparative-genomic basis for evaluating adaptation in Mars analogues and for prioritizing molecular hypotheses, while environmental drivers and realized resource use remain to be established.''' +s[end:]
s=s.replace('Keywords: Mars analogues; microbial strategies; environmental constraints; resource acquisition; phylogenomics; Bayesian analysis','Keywords: Mars analogues; microbial strategies; phylogenomics; cellular maintenance; energy acquisition; protein function prediction')
intro='''Three related questions connect environmental comparisons to molecular adaptation. First, do shared maintenance and energy-acquisition functions have similar distributions across lineages? Second, do source-associated differences reflect broad strategy categories or particular molecular systems? Third, can proteins outside assigned functional repertoires be resolved into candidate components of survival and resource-acquisition repertoires? Functional redundancy and thermodynamic convergence at community scale do not establish equivalent genetic implementations within organisms, nor independent evolutionary origins of shared traits [9, 10, 11]. The first two questions can be investigated with annotated genomic repertoires; the third requires additional sequence, structural and evolutionary evidence.

These questions operate at a common genomic scale but have different evidential requirements. Marker detection measures encoded potential rather than expression or flux. Atmospheric-gas oxidation requires more specific evidence than a generic hydrogenase or CO-associated annotation [12]. Conversely, a protein without an assigned KO need not lack recognizable domains or belong to a new biochemical class. AI-assisted function prediction can help prioritize such sequences for investigation [40, 41], while motif, domain, genomic-context and phylogenetic analyses determine which functional interpretation is supportable. A molecular candidate can be informative before its ecological effect is established.

Here we examine the lineage structure and molecular composition of microbial survival and resource-acquisition repertoires across seven source catalogues. We test how a maintenance–energy comparison changes when broad categories are resolved into specified gene combinations, evaluate additional dryland genomes for molecular and ecological support, and characterize the AI-prioritized DPFQ008 family as a complementary candidate-function case. These analyses distinguish the distribution of known capabilities from the interpretation of unassigned proteins. Environmental measurements and sampling provenance delimit the conditions under which their genomic organization can inform questions about adaptation.''' 
a=s.index('Three explanations consequently merit distinction.');b=s.index('\n\n## Results',a);s=s[:a]+intro+s[b:]
# Keep all existing primary numerical analyses, but give the molecular example its own result.
s=s.replace('### Source structure is concentrated in potassium-associated combinations','### Specific gene combinations resolve heterogeneous source associations')
